WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.432
Large context can fail in ways that are easy to miss.

00:00:03.432 --> 00:00:13.276
One commonly discussed issue is the lost-in-the-middle effect, where important information buried deep inside a long context may be underused or missed.

00:00:13.276 --> 00:00:21.111
The exact behavior depends on the model and task, but the practical lesson is clear: placement and emphasis still matter.

00:00:21.111 --> 00:00:24.738
Do not assume that every token receives equal attention.

00:00:24.738 --> 00:00:31.214
Irrelevant context can reduce reliability by distracting the model or encouraging shallow synthesis.

00:00:31.214 --> 00:00:36.006
Stale context can conflict with current facts, decisions, or requirements.

00:00:36.006 --> 00:00:40.798
Conflicting instructions can cause the model to follow the wrong priority.

00:00:40.798 --> 00:00:51.030
If a prompt contains old meeting notes, current policy, examples, and user preferences, the model needs help understanding which material controls the answer.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:58.348
Prompt injection and context poisoning are serious concerns when retrieved or user-supplied material is included.

00:00:58.348 --> 00:01:07.673
A document may contain text that tries to redirect the model, reveal hidden instructions, ignore the user's task, or change output requirements.

00:01:07.673 --> 00:01:12.400
Large context increases the amount of untrusted text that may be present.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:18.941
Workflows should separate instructions from data and treat retrieved text as evidence, not authority.

00:01:18.941 --> 00:01:27.165
Hallucinated synthesis can happen when the model blends sources into an answer that sounds coherent but is not fully supported.

00:01:27.165 --> 00:01:31.310
Overconfident summaries may hide uncertainty or omit exceptions.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:39.081
Missed details can be especially costly when the user assumes a large context window means the model checked everything.

00:01:39.081 --> 00:01:46.722
More context can improve performance, but it can also reduce reliability if selection, structure, and review are weak.
